@node	1.3.2, 1.3.3, 1.3.1, 1.3
@subsection Constructing Procedures Using @code{Lambda}

In using @code{sum} as in @ref{1.3.1}, it seems terribly awkward to have to
define trivial procedures such as @code{pi_term} and @code{pi_next} just so we
can use them as arguments to our higher-order procedure.  Rather than define
@code{pi_next} and @code{pi_term}, it would be more convenient to have a way to
directly specify ``the procedure that returns its input incremented by 4'' and
``the procedure that returns the reciprocal of its input times its input plus
2.''  We can do this by introducing the special form @code{fun}, OCaml's
anonymous-function form (the idea Scheme names @code{lambda}, the word this
subsection's heading still uses for it).  Using @code{fun} we can describe
what we want as

@example ocaml
fun x -> x +. 4.0;;
@end example

@noindent
and

@example ocaml
fun x -> 1.0 /. (x *. (x +. 2.0));;
@end example

@noindent
Then our @code{pi_sum} procedure can be expressed without defining any
auxiliary procedures as:

@example ocaml
let pi_sum a b =
  sum
    (fun x -> 1.0 /. (x *. (x +. 2.0)))
    (Float.of_int a)
    (fun x -> x +. 4.0)
    (Float.of_int b);;
@end example

@noindent
Again using @code{fun}, we can write the @code{integral} procedure without
having to define the auxiliary procedure @code{add_dx}:

@example ocaml
let integral f a b dx =
  sum f (a +. (dx /. 2.0)) (fun x -> x +. dx) b *. dx;;
@end example

@noindent
In general, @code{fun} is used to create procedures in the same way as
@code{let}, except that no name is specified for the procedure:

@example ocaml
fun ⟨formal-parameters⟩ -> ⟨body⟩;;
@end example

@noindent
The resulting procedure is just as much a procedure as one that is created
using @code{let}.  The only difference is that it has not been associated with
any name in the environment.  In fact,

@example ocaml
let plus4 x = x + 4;;
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to

@example ocaml
let plus4 = fun x -> x + 4;;
@end example

@noindent
We can read a @code{fun} expression as follows:

@example
(fun    x    ->   x   +   4)
   |    |          |   |   |
the procedure of an argument x that adds x and 4
@end example

@noindent
Like any expression that has a procedure as its value, a @code{fun} expression
can be used as the operator in a combination such as (here @code{square} is
redefined for integers, shadowing the floating-point version of
@ref{1.1.7}):

@example ocaml
let square x = x * x;;

(fun x y z -> x + y + square z) 1 2 3;;
(* => 12 *)
@end example

@noindent
or, more generally, in any context where we would normally use a procedure
name.@footnote{It would be clearer and less intimidating to people learning
Lisp if a name more obvious than @code{lambda}, such as @code{make-procedure},
were used.  But the convention is firmly entrenched, and OCaml's own
@code{fun} keeps it alive in spirit if not in spelling.  The notation is
adopted from the λ-calculus, a mathematical formalism introduced by the
mathematical logician Alonzo @ref{Church (1941)}.  Church developed the
λ-calculus to provide a rigorous foundation for studying the notions of
function and function application.  The λ-calculus has become a basic tool
for mathematical investigations of the semantics of programming
languages---including, decades later, OCaml's own.}

@subsubheading Using @code{let} to create local variables

Another use of @code{fun}, wrapped in a @code{let}, is in creating local
variables.  We often need local variables in our procedures other than those
that have been bound as formal parameters.  For example, suppose we wish to
compute the function
@displaymath
{f(x,y)} \,=\, {x(1 + xy)^2} + {y(1 - y)} + {(1 + xy)(1 - y),}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
f(x,y) = x(1 + xy)^2 + y(1 - y) + (1 + xy)(1 - y)
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
which we could also express as
@displaymath
\eqalign{
  a                     &=   {1 + xy,}  \cr
  \hphantom{(x,y)} b    &=   {1 - y,}   \cr
  {f(x,y)}              &=   {xa^2} + {yb} + {ab.}
}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
     a = 1 + xy
     b = 1 - y
f(x,y) = xa^2 + yb + ab
@end example

@end ifinfo
In writing a procedure to compute @math{f}, we would like to include as local
variables not only @math{x} and @math{y} but also the names of intermediate
quantities like @math{a} and @math{b}.  One way to accomplish this is to use an
auxiliary procedure to bind the local variables:

@example ocaml
let f x y =
  let f_helper a b = (x *. (a *. a)) +. (y *. b) +. (a *. b) in
  f_helper (1.0 +. (x *. y)) (1.0 -. y);;
@end example

@noindent
Of course, we could use a @code{fun} expression to specify an anonymous
procedure for binding our local variables.  The body of @code{f} then becomes a
single call to that procedure:

@example ocaml
let f x y =
  (fun a b -> (x *. (a *. a)) +. (y *. b) +. (a *. b))
    (1.0 +. (x *. y))
    (1.0 -. y);;
@end example

@noindent
This construct is so useful that there is a special form called @code{let} that
already makes its use convenient---indeed, this is the same @code{let} used
throughout the book so far to name intermediate results.  Written the direct
way, the @code{f} procedure could be

@example ocaml
let f x y =
  let a = 1.0 +. (x *. y) in
  let b = 1.0 -. y in
  (x *. (a *. a)) +. (y *. b) +. (a *. b);;
@end example

@noindent
The general form of a @code{let} expression is

@example ocaml
let ⟨var₁⟩ = ⟨exp₁⟩ in
let ⟨var₂⟩ = ⟨exp₂⟩ in
…
let ⟨varₙ⟩ = ⟨expₙ⟩ in
⟨body⟩
@end example

@noindent
which can be thought of as saying

@example
let ⟨var₁⟩ have the value ⟨exp₁⟩ and
    ⟨var₂⟩ have the value ⟨exp₂⟩ and
    …
    ⟨varₙ⟩ have the value ⟨expₙ⟩
  in ⟨body⟩
@end example

@noindent
Each @code{let} binds one name to the value of the expression that follows
@code{=}; the name is in scope for everything after its own @code{in}, which
in this multi-binding shape is the next @code{let}, nested one inside the
other.  No new evaluation mechanism is required to provide local variables:
@code{let x = e in body} is exactly the function application @code{(fun x ->
body) e}, so a chain of @code{let}s is a chain of nested one-argument
applications.

We can see from this equivalence that the scope of a variable specified by a
@code{let} expression is the @code{in body} that follows it.  This implies
that:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Let} allows one to bind variables as locally as possible to where they
are to be used.  For example, if the value of @code{x} is 5, the value of the
expression

@example ocaml
(let x = 3 in
 x +. (x *. 10.0)) +. outer_x;;
@end example

@noindent
is 38 when @code{outer_x} is 5.0.  Here, the @code{x} in the body of the
@code{let} is 3, so the value of the @code{let} expression is 33.  On the
other hand, the @code{x} named @code{outer_x} outside the @code{let} is still
5, untouched by the inner binding.

@item
The variables' values are computed outside the @code{let}.  This matters when
the expressions that provide the values for the local variables depend upon
variables having the same names as the local variables themselves.  For
example, if the value of @code{outer_x} is 2, the expression

@example ocaml
let x = 3.0 in
let y = outer_x +. 2.0 in
x *. y;;
@end example

@noindent
will have the value 12 because, inside the body, @code{x} will be 3 and
@code{y} will be 4 (which is the outer @code{x} plus 2): the second
@code{let}'s right-hand side is evaluated before its own name comes into
scope, so it still sees the outer @code{x}, exactly as the first @code{let}'s
right-hand side did.

@end itemize

@noindent
Sometimes we can use internal definitions to get the same effect as with
@code{let}.  For example, we could have defined the procedure @code{f} above
with two internal @code{let}s instead of one @code{let} binding a pair:

@example ocaml
let f x y =
  let a = 1.0 +. (x *. y) in
  let b = 1.0 -. y in
  (x *. (a *. a)) +. (y *. b) +. (a *. b);;
@end example

@noindent
which is in fact identical to the version already shown above.  We prefer,
however, to bind @code{a} and @code{b} together in one @code{let} as shown
earlier, and to reach for two separate @code{let}s like this mainly when one
of them names a helper procedure rather than a plain value.@footnote{OCaml
has no separate ``internal @code{define}'' form the way Scheme does: every
internal binding, whether it names a value or a procedure, is a @code{let}
that sees only what came before it in the same body, and a self-referential
one says so explicitly with @code{let rec}.  This is simpler than Scheme's
internal-definition model, where a block of internal @code{define}s all come
into scope together before any of their bodies run.  Understanding internal
definitions well enough to be sure a program means what we intend it to mean
requires a more elaborate model of the evaluation process than we have
presented in this chapter; OCaml's ordinary sequential @code{let} scoping
avoids that subtlety entirely, since there is only the one binding form.  We
will return to this issue in @ref{4.1.6}, after we learn more about
evaluation.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.34}Exercise 1.34:} Suppose we define the procedures

@example ocaml
let square x = x * x;;

let f g = g 2;;
@end example

@noindent
Then we have

@example ocaml
f square;;
(* => 4 *)

f (fun z -> z * (z + 1));;
(* => 6 *)
@end example

@noindent
What happens if we (perversely) ask the compiler to type-check the combination
@code{f f}?  Explain, in terms of the type OCaml infers for @code{f} from the
two calls above.
@end quotation

@node 1.3.3, 1.3.4, 1.3.2, 1.3
@subsection Procedures as General Methods

We introduced compound procedures in @ref{1.1.4} as a mechanism for
abstracting patterns of numerical operations so as to make them independent of
the particular numbers involved.  With higher-order procedures, such as the
@code{integral} procedure of @ref{1.3.1}, we began to see a more powerful kind
of abstraction: procedures used to express general methods of computation,
independent of the particular functions involved.  In this section we discuss
two more elaborate examples---general methods for finding zeros and fixed
points of functions---and show how these methods can be expressed directly as
procedures.

@subsubheading Finding roots of equations by the half-interval method

The @newterm{half-interval method} is a simple but powerful technique for
finding roots of an equation @math{{f(x) = 0}}, where @math{f} is a continuous
function.  The idea is that, if we are given points @math{a} and @math{b} such
that @math{{f(a) < 0 < f(b)}}, then @math{f} must have at least one zero
between @math{a} and @math{b}.  To locate a zero, let @math{x} be the average of
@math{a} and @math{b} and compute @math{f(x)}.  If @math{{f(x) > 0}}, then
@math{f} must have a zero between @math{a} and @math{x}.  If @math{{f(x) <
0}}, then @math{f} must have a zero between @math{x} and @math{b}.  Continuing
in this way, we can identify smaller and smaller intervals on which @math{f}
must have a zero.  When we reach a point where the interval is small enough,
the process stops.  Since the uncertainty is reduced by half at each step of
the process, the number of steps required grows as @math{{\Theta(\log(L\,
/\, T))}}, where @math{L} is the length of the original interval and @math{T} is
the error tolerance (that is, the size of the interval we will consider
``small enough'').  Here is a procedure that implements this strategy:

@example ocaml
let close_enough a b = Float.abs (a -. b) < 0.001;;

let rec search f neg_point pos_point =
  let midpoint = (neg_point +. pos_point) /. 2.0 in
  if close_enough neg_point pos_point
  then midpoint
  else (
    let test_value = f midpoint in
    if test_value > 0.0
    then search f neg_point midpoint
    else if test_value < 0.0
    then search f midpoint pos_point
    else midpoint);;
@end example

@noindent
We assume that we are initially given the function @math{f} together with
points at which its values are negative and positive.  We first compute the
midpoint of the two given points.  Next we check to see if the given interval
is small enough, and if so we simply return the midpoint as our answer.
Otherwise, we compute as a test value the value of @math{f} at the midpoint.
If the test value is positive, then we continue the process with a new
interval running from the original negative point to the midpoint.  If the
test value is negative, we continue with the interval from the midpoint to the
original positive point.  Finally, there is the possibility that the test
value is 0, in which case the midpoint is itself the root we are searching
for.

To test whether the endpoints are ``close enough'' we can use a tolerance
similar to the one used in @ref{1.1.7} for computing square
roots:@footnote{We have used 0.001 as a representative ``small'' number to
indicate a tolerance for the acceptable error in a calculation.  The
appropriate tolerance for a real calculation depends upon the problem to be
solved and the limitations of the computer and the algorithm.  This is often a
very subtle consideration, requiring help from a numerical analyst or some
other kind of magician.}

@code{Search} is awkward to use directly, because we can accidentally give it
points at which @math{f}'s values do not have the required sign, in which case
we get a wrong answer.  Instead we will use @code{search} via the following
procedure, which checks to see which of the endpoints has a negative function
value and which has a positive value, and calls @code{search} accordingly.  If
the function has the same sign on the two given points, the half-interval
method cannot be used, in which case the procedure returns an
@code{Error}.@footnote{Scheme signals this with @code{error}, which takes as
arguments a number of items that are printed as error messages.  This edition
returns @code{Error Values_not_of_opposite_sign} instead, the typed way of
reporting a recoverable failure that the rest of the book uses throughout.}

@example ocaml
let half_interval_method f a b =
  let a_value = f a in
  let b_value = f b in
  if a_value < 0.0 && b_value > 0.0
  then Ok (search f a b)
  else if b_value < 0.0 && a_value > 0.0
  then Ok (search f b a)
  else Error Values_not_of_opposite_sign;;
@end example

@noindent
The following example uses the half-interval method to approximate @math{\pi} as
the root between 2 and 4 of @math{{\sin x = 0}}:

@example ocaml
half_interval_method sin 2.0 4.0;;
(* => Ok 3.14111328125 *)
@end example

@noindent
Here is another example, using the half-interval method to search for a root of
the equation @math{{x^3 - 2x - 3 = 0}} between 1 and 2:

@example ocaml
half_interval_method
  (fun x -> (x *. x *. x) -. (2.0 *. x) -. 3.0)
  1.0 2.0;;
(* => Ok 1.89306640625 *)
@end example

@subsubheading Finding fixed points of functions

A number @math{x} is called a @newterm{fixed point} of a function @math{f} if @math{x}
satisfies the equation @math{{f(x) = x}}.  For some functions @math{f} we can
locate a fixed point by beginning with an initial guess and applying @math{f}
repeatedly,
@displaymath
f(x),\quad f(f(x)),\quad f(f(f(x))), \quad\dots,
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
f(x), f(f(x)), f(f(f(x))), ...
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
until the value does not change very much.  Using this idea, we can devise a
procedure @code{fixed_point} that takes as inputs a function and an initial
guess and produces an approximation to a fixed point of the function.  We apply
the function repeatedly until we find two successive values whose difference is
less than some prescribed tolerance:

@example ocaml
let tolerance = 0.00001;;

let fixed_point ?(max_iterations = 100_000) f first_guess =
  let close_enough v1 v2 = Float.abs (v1 -. v2) < tolerance in
  let rec try_guess guess n =
    if n > max_iterations
    then Error Not_converged
    else (
      let next = f guess in
      if close_enough guess next
      then Ok next
      else try_guess next (n + 1))
  in
  try_guess first_guess 0;;
@end example

@noindent
The @code{max_iterations} bound is this edition's own addition, with no
Scheme counterpart: a search that never settles, which this same subsection
meets two pages on, would otherwise recurse without limit, and a batch build
has no way to interrupt a runaway process the way pressing a key at an
interactive REPL does.  Bounding the search turns that non-termination into an
ordinary, observable @code{Error Not_converged} instead.

For example, we can use this method to approximate the fixed point of the
cosine function, starting with 1 as an initial approximation:@footnote{Try this
during a boring lecture: Set your calculator to radians mode and then
repeatedly press the @code{cos} button until you obtain the fixed point.}

@example ocaml
fixed_point cos 1.0;;
(* => Ok 0.7390822985224024 *)
@end example

@noindent
Similarly, we can find a solution to the equation
@math{{y = \sin y + \cos y}}:

@example ocaml
fixed_point (fun y -> sin y +. cos y) 1.0;;
(* => Ok 1.2587315962971173 *)
@end example

@noindent
The fixed-point process is reminiscent of the process we used for finding
square roots in @ref{1.1.7}.  Both are based on the idea of repeatedly
improving a guess until the result satisfies some criterion.  In fact, we can
readily formulate the square-root computation as a fixed-point search.
Computing the square root of some number @math{x} requires finding a @math{y}
such that @math{{y^2 = x}}.  Putting this equation into the equivalent form
@math{{y = x / y}}, we recognize that we are looking for a fixed point of the
function@footnote{@math{y \mapsto x / y} (pronounced ``@math{y} maps to
@math{x / y}'') means the function whose value at @math{y} is @math{x /
y}.}@math{{y \mapsto x / y}}, and we can therefore try to compute square roots
as

@example ocaml
fixed_point (fun y -> x /. y) 1.0;;
@end example

@noindent
Unfortunately, this fixed-point search does not converge.  Consider an initial
guess @math{{y_1}}.  The next guess is @math{{y_2} = x / {y_1}} and the next
guess is @math{{y_3} = x / {y_2} = x / (x / {y_1}) = {y_1}}.  This results in an
infinite loop in which @math{y_1}, @math{y_2}, @math{y_1}, @math{y_2}, @dots{}
repeat over and over, oscillating about the answer.  One way to control such
oscillations is to prevent the guesses from changing so much.  Since the answer
is always between our guess @math{y} and @math{{x / y}}, we can make a new
guess that is not as far from @math{y} as @math{{x / y}} by averaging @math{y}
with @math{{x / y}}, so that the next guess after @math{y} is @math{{1\over2}(y
+ x / y)} instead of @math{{x / y}}.  The process of making such a sequence of
guesses is simply the process of looking for a fixed point of @math{{y \mapsto
{1\over2}(y + x / y)}}:

@example ocaml
fixed_point (fun y -> (y +. (x /. y)) /. 2.0) 1.0;;
@end example

@noindent
(Note that @math{{y = {1\over2}(y + x / y)}} is a simple transformation of the
equation @math{{y = x / y}}; to derive it, add @math{y} to both sides of the
equation and divide by 2.)

With this modification, the square-root procedure works.  In fact, if we
unravel the definitions, we can see that the sequence of approximations to the
square root generated here is precisely the same as the one generated by our
original square-root procedure of @ref{1.1.7}.  This approach of averaging
successive approximations to a solution, a technique we call
@newterm{average damping}, often aids the convergence of fixed-point searches.

@code{fixed_point} returns @code{Error Not_converged} rather than looping
forever when a search like the undamped one above is actually run against the
edition's iteration cap; the average-damped version instead settles, as this
section's runnable listings demonstrate for the definitions given in
@ref{1.3.4}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.35}Exercise 1.35:} Show that the golden ratio
@math{\varphi} (@ref{1.2.2}) is a fixed point of the transformation
@math{{x \mapsto 1 + 1 / x}}, and use this fact to compute @math{\varphi} by
means of the @code{fixed_point} procedure.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.36}Exercise 1.36:} Modify @code{fixed_point} so that
it prints the sequence of approximations it generates, using the
@code{print_endline} primitive shown in @ref{Exercise 1.22}.  Then find a
solution to @math{{x^x = 1000}} by finding a fixed point of
@math{{x \mapsto \log 1000 / \log x}}.  (Use OCaml's primitive @code{log}
function, which computes natural logarithms.)  Compare the number of steps
this takes with and without average damping.  (Note that you cannot start
@code{fixed_point} with a guess of 1: OCaml's
@code{1.0 /. 0.0} does not raise an error---it returns @code{infinity}---and a
search that never leaves @code{infinity} behind is stuck just the same until
the edition's iteration cap turns that stall into @code{Error Not_converged}.)
@end quotation

@quotation
